# config: mock-eval.json
#
# Reviewing an engine's move is what the evaluation overlay is for — ADR-0007
# decision 7, analysis follows the review cursor, is the decision that justifies
# giving it its own process at all. It did not work.
#
# analysisMayRun() yielded on `isRunning() && isCurrentPlayerEngine()`, and both
# halves misread the situation during review: isCurrentPlayerEngine() asks about
# the colour to move *under the cursor*, and isRunning() only asks whether the
# loop thread exists — which it does, sitting in its `phase() != Playing`
# early-out, searching nothing. So stepping back onto the move an engine had just
# played took the overlay away, in the one place it was worth most. The only way
# to see it was to swap the players.
#
# The predicate is the phase now. Decision 6's yield is unchanged and asserted
# below, because a match in progress really does keep the device.
#
#   ./tests/run_scenarios.sh tests/scenarios/evaluation_reviews_an_engine_move.scn

new_game 9
wait_idle
switch_player black human
switch_player white mock
wait_idle

toggle_evaluation on
wait_until eval_state running

# One move each: the human's, then the mock's reply. Black is to move at the end
# of the line, and the mock reports 0.6 for whoever that is.
click 2 2
click 2 2
wait_until move_count >= 2
expect at_end true
expect color_to_move B
wait_until eval_winrate 60

# Back onto the position the mock answered. White to move there, and White *is*
# the mock — the exact shape that used to silence the stream. Nothing is
# thinking: navigating called enterReview(), so the game is paused.
navigate_back
wait_idle
expect view_position 1
expect phase paused
expect color_to_move W
expect thinking false

# The regression. `running` rather than `yielded`, and a live report for the
# position under the cursor — 40, because the mock's 0.6 is for White here and
# the client anchors to Black.
wait_until eval_state running
wait_until eval_winrate 40
expect eval_stale false

navigate_end
wait_idle
expect at_end true
wait_until eval_winrate 60

# Both sides the mock, still paused. Still analysed: the question is whether a
# game is being *played*, not who the players are — a bot-versus-bot game being
# reviewed is as much a review as any other.
switch_player black mock
wait_idle
expect phase paused
wait_until eval_state running

# And the yield is intact (ADR-0007 decision 6). Once the match is running with
# an engine to move the stream stands down and stays down, including the 500 ms
# the loop sleeps between moves with playerToMove already cleared — which is why
# this term cannot be a bare isThinking() and must survive any future tidying.
start
wait_until eval_state yielded
